This research aims to help non-technical users understand how HP docking station models have evolved.
This is done by showing how their features affect laptop compatibility and connectivity. This research will help with identifying and resolving common 
docking station issues. 


HP docking station 

1. HP Native docking station

HP USB-C docking stations have supported display connections, peripherals, Ethernet cable ports, and power delivery through 
a USB-C connection.

Examples: 

-HP Elite USB-C Dock: This is the earlier USB-C docking station designed to expand connectivity 
for compatibility with HP laptops.

-HP USB-C DOCK G5: This is a more versatile USB-C docking station designed to support HP and non-HP
laptops. 

Sources:

-https://support.hp.com/us-en/product/product-specs/hp-elite-usb-c-dock/model/15223699
-https://support.hp.com/us-en/product/product-specs/usb-c-docking/model/27767206

2. Universal USB-C/ Hybrid Docks
 Hybrid docks are flexible docking stations built to work for any laptop. They connect via USB-C or an included USB-A adapter.
IT uses DisplayLink technology to provide display connectivity and peripheral access across different laptops. 


Examples:
HP USB-C/A Universal Dock G2:
Connects natively via USB-C or through a USB-A adapter. 
Supports up to three 4K displays when operating over USB-C,
or up to two 4K displays when attached via USB-A.
USB-C connections support host Power Delivery; 
legacy USB-A connections are limited strictly to data transfer 
and video output without host charging.


Display support:

USB-C connection: Supports up to three 4K displays, depending on host capabilities.

USB-A connection: Supports up to two 4K displays, depending on host capabilities.

Sources:
-https://h20195.www2.hp.com/v2/GetDocument.aspx?docname=4AA7-5549ENW

-https://www.hp.com/us-en/monitors-accessories/computer-accessories/overlays/usb-c-a-universal-dock-g2.html

3. Thunderbolt Docking Stations
Thunderbolt docks are heavy-duty docking stations that are built for high-performance laptops. They can handle larger amounts of data and power than 
the standard USB-C docks, which makes them ideal for running multiple high-resolution monitors or transferring larger files. 

Examples: 
HP Thunderbolt Dock G2/G4: These are square, compact docking stations that connect using a thick, high-speed Thunderbolt cable. 

Sources:

-https://support.hp.com/ca-en/document/ish_6213504-6213991-16

Troubleshooting Checks:

Identify the docking station model and verify its compatibility with the laptop.

Check that the power adapter and connecting cables are securely connected.

Confirm that the laptop's USB-C or Thunderbolt port supports the features required by the docking station.

If external monitors are not working, check the monitor's selected input, cable connections, and supported display configuration.

Verify that the required drivers, firmware, and docking station updates are installed.

